-## Subtopics
-
-### Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF)
-A method that represents opacity as a continuous field, differing from discrete Gaussian splats. It is chosen when smooth opacity transitions are required without discrete primitives. See [Gaussian Opacity Fields (GOF)](5992-gaussian-opacity-fields-gof.md).
-
-### QuadBox
-A method that approximates Gaussian splats with axis-aligned bounding boxes for faster rasterization. It is chosen when performance is critical and slight accuracy trade-offs are acceptable. See [QuadBox](9231-quadbox.md).
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method incurs reduced setup costs per Gaussian compared to methods requiring matrix inversion but slightly higher costs per pixel due to the Plücker coordinate calculations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the transformation matrix $T'$ and Plücker coordinates, but avoids storing large intermediate matrices for inversion [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** The source notes that the tradeoff is desirable when graphics primitives cover only few pixels [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Numerically stable for degenerate splats (vanishing axes), no matrix inversion required, closed-form bounding box calculation [S1].
